---
title: Hand the route to another keeper
lang: en
---

## Make the current entry clear

By the end of this lesson, you can give a handoff that names the current visit, preserves corrections and identifies an unresolved question.

The incoming keeper needs to know which instruction is current. Begin with the station name and visit number. State the shutter condition, then the next action. If you corrected an earlier reply, say so. Leave both wordings in the ledger so the incoming keeper can follow the change.

An [unresolved question]{A question that the current visit has not answered.} travels with the handoff instead of becoming an assumed fact.

--- Compare handoff notes

## Compare handoff notes

### Short but incomplete
as: tab

"Still waiting." This gives no station, visit or current shutter condition.

### Specific and current

"Moth Bend, visit five, amber. Hold the routine reply until a fresh instruction arrives. Earlier release reply corrected."

### Question carried forward

"We do not yet know when the fresh instruction will arrive. Ask the route keeper at the next visit."

close: tab

A handoff can be brief. It still needs enough detail to identify the instruction that the next keeper should use.

--- Find the missing detail

## Find the missing detail

Your note says "Moth Bend, amber; hold the routine reply." The incoming keeper finds two entries with that wording from different visits.

### Which detail would distinguish the current entry?
as: choice

(x) The visit number
feedback: Visit numbers distinguish separate arrivals at the same station.
( ) The colour of your pocket book
feedback: The book colour does not identify the current visit.
( ) A longer explanation of amber
feedback: Both entries already name the same state.

--- Rehearse a handoff

## Rehearse a handoff

Use the entry you saved in the ledger lesson. Give a spoken handoff to a partner, or exchange written notes. Include an unresolved question.

Ask your partner to repeat the current instruction and identify the visit. Compare their reply with your entry. If it differs, revise the handoff wording and try again.

Write a reflection about the detail that was easiest to lose. There is no keyed answer; the useful evidence is what your partner could recover from your wording.
